  "body": " Sheaf transfer   Let be a commutative square of complete Tate Huber pairs. For , write for the induced map. For a rational domain , put , and set . Suppose that for every rational domain the section rings satisfy a natural strict exact sequence If the Huber pairs associated with are sheafy as complete topological rings, then the Huber pair associated with is sheafy as a complete topological ring.    We first work on the rational basis. Let be a finite rational covering. For , let denote product restriction, using and as specified in the statement. The first map in is a topological embedding, and the same is true after replacing by any . Since and are sheafy, and are topological embeddings. Naturality gives a commutative square The top and right arrows are topological embeddings, as is the bottom arrow. It follows that is a topological embedding.  Now let be a matching family. Its images in the - and -section rings glue to elements Their images in agree after restriction to every . Since the structure presheaf for is separated, they agree globally. Exactness on therefore gives mapping to . Exactness on each shows that , and uniqueness follows in the same way. Thus identifies homeomorphically with the matching-family equalizer, which is closed in the finite product because all section rings are Hausdorff.  The underlying presheaf is therefore a sheaf by the sheaf-on-a-basis theorem . To retain the topology on arbitrary opens, let be an open covering and let be its matching-family equalizer with the subspace topology. For every rational , quasi-compactness gives a finite rational cover of subordinate to the . Restriction from to that finite cover, followed by the rational-basis result, gives a continuous map , independent of the chosen subordinate cover. These maps are compatible under rational restriction. Since with its projective-limit topology, the inverse of the algebraic gluing map is continuous. Hence the gluing map is a homeomorphism, as required.   "
